From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:59:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: Optionally keep the compute kick to a
 queue alone

g16_submit_defer, g16_submit_kick and g16_submit_defer_render move a
compute submission that finds a few compute flights out onto a poll
pass that the submission asks for at once. Alone, that pass publishes
several records together. Beside another client the kicked pass can
take the firmware just before the other client's record lands, and the
other client waits a pass. Measured on linux-aurora-next 1.1093 with
the three words at 2, 16 and 16 against 16, 0 and 0, on one boot:

  render.light depth 1 beside compute.light depth 4:
    307.67 -> 314.99 us per submission (+2.4 %, slower in 5 of 6
    block pairs)
  compute.light depth 1 beside compute.light depth 8:
    408.80 -> 419.88 us per submission (+2.7 %)

On another boot of 1.1093, compute.light alone at depth 8 went from
32.64 to 29.85 us per submission.

Add g16_submit_solo_us = W (runtime 0644, default 0). With W set, the
three words act as set only for a compute submission whose queue is
alone: every flight in the engine is its own, and no other queue has
submitted, published or retired work within the last W us. Otherwise
the submission takes the old rule, queued only from 16 compute flights
up and never kicked. A queue is one client, so two processes and two
queues of one process are both refused.

The flights check shares the walk the submission already makes to
count compute flights, which now also finds a render flight, so a
submission walks the flights once instead of twice. The engine keeps
the latest activity and the latest activity of another queue, which
answers the window in constant time. A stats line counts the
decisions: alone, kicked alone, refused for a flight of another queue
and refused inside the window.

The rule is in a core-only module with host tests. Nothing changes
while the word is 0.

Assisted-by: LLM
